\section{The image document}

A document is decoded once, when it is opened, into three things: the float
[[PixelBuffer]] all analysis works on, a [[CGImage]] made \emph{from that buffer} for display,
and a record of facts about the file. Displaying the buffer's own rendering, not the decoded file
image, guarantees that what the user sees is exactly the data being analyzed.

<<LineScope/Document/ImageDocument.swift>>=
import ImageIO
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI
import UniformTypeIdentifiers

<<ImageDocument: source information>>

<<ImageDocument: the document type>>
@

The facts are gathered before the conversion to 8-bit sRGB can hide them (Chapter~\ref{ch:pixels}):
the original bit depth, color space and alpha. The inspector shows them, so a user who opened a
16-bit or CMYK file knows it was converted.

<<ImageDocument: source information>>=
/// Facts about the file as decoded, before conversion to the working float buffer.
struct SourceInfo {
    var fileType: String
    var pixelWidth: Int
    var pixelHeight: Int
    var bitsPerComponent: Int
    var bitsPerPixel: Int
    var colorSpaceName: String
    var hasAlpha: Bool
    var dpi: Double?
}
@

The document accepts any image type ImageIO can read. It is a value type holding immutable data,
which is all [[FileDocument]] needs for viewing.

<<ImageDocument: the document type>>=
/// Read-only image document. Pixels are decoded once into a `PixelBuffer`; EXIF orientation is ignored
/// on purpose so analysis runs on the stored pixel grid.
struct ImageDocument: FileDocument {
    static var readableContentTypes: [UTType] { [.image] }

    let buffer: PixelBuffer
    let displayImage: CGImage
    let info: SourceInfo

    <<ImageDocument: reading a file>>

    <<ImageDocument: a generated test pattern>>

    <<ImageDocument: refusing to write>>
}
@

Reading takes the first image of the file, converts it and renders it back. Any failure along the
chain is reported as a corrupt file, which makes the system show its standard error alert.

One decision is visible only by its absence: the EXIF orientation tag is \emph{not} applied. A photo
taken in portrait may therefore appear rotated. Applying the tag would mean rotating or mirroring the
pixel grid, and LineScope's purpose is to analyze the grid exactly as stored.

<<ImageDocument: reading a file>>=
init(configuration: ReadConfiguration) throws {
    <<ImageDocument: decoding the first image>>

    <<ImageDocument: recording facts about the source>>
}
@

<<ImageDocument: decoding the first image>>=
guard
    let data = configuration.file.regularFileContents,
    let source = CGImageSourceCreateWithData(data as CFData, nil),
    let cg = CGImageSourceCreateImageAtIndex(source, 0, nil),
    let buffer = PixelBuffer(cgImage: cg),
    let display = buffer.makeCGImage()
else { throw CocoaError(.fileReadCorruptFile) }
@

The file type comes from ImageIO's type identifier, turned into a readable description such as
``PNG image''. The color space name loses its [[kCGColorSpace]] prefix. An image has alpha unless its
alpha info says the fourth channel is absent or to be skipped.

<<ImageDocument: recording facts about the source>>=
let props = CGImageSourceCopyPropertiesAtIndex(source, 0, nil) as? [CFString: Any]
let type = (CGImageSourceGetType(source) as String?).flatMap { UTType($0)?.localizedDescription }
let alpha = cg.alphaInfo
self.buffer = buffer
self.displayImage = display
self.info = SourceInfo(
    fileType: type ?? "Image",
    pixelWidth: cg.width,
    pixelHeight: cg.height,
    bitsPerComponent: cg.bitsPerComponent,
    bitsPerPixel: cg.bitsPerPixel,
    colorSpaceName: (cg.colorSpace?.name as String?)?.replacingOccurrences(of: "kCGColorSpace", with: "") ?? "Unknown",
    hasAlpha: !(alpha == .none || alpha == .noneSkipFirst || alpha == .noneSkipLast),
    dpi: props?[kCGImagePropertyDPIWidth] as? Double
)
@

A generated pattern becomes a document too, so that it can be shown in exactly the same window as a
file. Its facts describe the float buffer itself: 32 bits per component, 128 per pixel, and no
alpha.

<<ImageDocument: a generated test pattern>>=
/// A generated test pattern, shown in the same analysis window as a file.
init?(pattern spec: TestPatternSpec) {
    let buffer = TestPatternGenerator.make(spec)
    guard let display = buffer.makeCGImage() else { return nil }
    self.buffer = buffer
    self.displayImage = display
    self.info = SourceInfo(
        fileType: "Generated test pattern",
        pixelWidth: buffer.width,
        pixelHeight: buffer.height,
        bitsPerComponent: 32,
        bitsPerPixel: 128,
        colorSpaceName: "sRGB (float)",
        hasAlpha: false,
        dpi: nil
    )
}
@

[[FileDocument]] requires a way to write, even for a viewer. It is never called, because the
viewing document group offers no Save command; derived images leave the program through Export
instead (Section~\ref{sec:export}).

<<ImageDocument: refusing to write>>=
func fileWrapper(configuration: WriteConfiguration) throws -> FileWrapper {
    throw CocoaError(.featureUnsupported)
}
