\section{Variants, zones and panes}

The next file holds the small types the session is made of.

<<LineScope/Model/ImageVariant.swift>>=
import CoreGraphics
import Foundation
import LineScopeCore

<<ImageVariant: the variant type>>

<<ImageVariant: zones>>

<<ImageVariant: panes>>

<<ImageVariant: the frequency axis>>

<<ImageVariant: operation requests>>
@

A \emph{variant} is the original image or one copy derived from it. Besides its pixels and their
rendering, it remembers its parent and the operation that produced it, and the whole
\emph{lineage}: the operations from the original down to this copy. Storing the lineage, rather than
walking parent links, keeps a variant's history intact when an intermediate tab is closed. A
variant's name, shown on its tab, is the lineage joined with arrows.

Variants are immutable. An operation never changes a variant; it creates a new one.

<<ImageVariant: the variant type>>=
/// The original image or one derived copy. Each copy records the chain of operations back to the original.
struct ImageVariant: Identifiable {
    let id = UUID()
    let buffer: PixelBuffer
    let cgImage: CGImage
    let parentID: UUID?
    let operation: ImageOperation?
    /// Operations applied to the original, in order. Empty for the original.
    let lineage: [ImageOperation]

    var isOriginal: Bool { operation == nil }

    var name: String {
        lineage.isEmpty ? "Original" : lineage.map(\.displayName).joined(separator: " → ")
    }
}
@

The window is divided into three horizontal \emph{zones}. The raw values index the session's pane
arrays.

<<ImageVariant: zones>>=
/// Which kind of content a zone shows.
enum Zone: Int, CaseIterable, Identifiable {
    case images, profile, spectrum

    var id: Int { rawValue }

    var title: String {
        switch self {
        case .images: "Images"
        case .profile: "Line Profile"
        case .spectrum: "Spectrum"
        }
    }
}
@

Each zone can be split into several side-by-side \emph{panes}, each showing one variant. A pane has
its own identity, separate from the variant it shows, so SwiftUI keeps a pane's view state (such
as its scroll position) when the pane switches to another variant.

<<ImageVariant: panes>>=
/// One side-by-side pane inside a zone; it shows one variant at a time.
struct PaneState: Identifiable, Equatable {
    let id = UUID()
    var variantID: UUID
}
@

The spectrum can be plotted against two frequency scales, discussed with the analysis in
Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis}: cycles per pixel of the variant itself, or cycles along the whole line,
which is the same for every copy.

<<ImageVariant: the frequency axis>>=
enum FrequencyAxis: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {
    case cyclesPerPixel
    case cyclesPerLine

    var id: String { rawValue }
    var displayName: String { self == .cyclesPerPixel ? "cycles/pixel" : "cycles/line" }
}
@

Some menu commands need parameters before they can run: a blur radius, a scale factor. Such a
command doesn't run the operation; it stores an [[OperationRequest]] in the session, and the window
presents a sheet for it. The request is [[Identifiable]] because SwiftUI's [[sheet(item:)]] requires
it.

<<ImageVariant: operation requests>>=
/// Operations that need parameters before running; shown as a sheet.
enum OperationRequest: Identifiable {
    case gaussianBlur, boxBlur, unsharpMask, noiseReduction
    case resample(ResampleMethod)

    var id: String {
        switch self {
        case .gaussianBlur: "gaussian"
        case .boxBlur: "box"
        case .unsharpMask: "unsharp"
        case .noiseReduction: "noise"
        case .resample(let m): "resample-\(m.rawValue)"
        }
    }

    var title: String {
        switch self {
        case .gaussianBlur: "Gaussian Blur"
        case .boxBlur: "Box Blur"
        case .unsharpMask: "Sharpen (Unsharp Mask)"
        case .noiseReduction: "Noise Reduction"
        case .resample(let m): "Resample: \(m.displayName)"
        }
    }
}
@

\section{The session}

Every window has one [[Session]]. It owns the variants, the shared line, the analysis settings,
the pane layout and the focus, and it performs operations. The views hold no state of their own
beyond transient gestures; they read the session and write to it.

The class is marked [[@Observable]]. SwiftUI records which properties each view's body reads, and
re-renders exactly those views when one of the properties changes. This is what keeps the line in
sync everywhere: dragging it in one pane writes [[line]], and every pane in all three zones read
[[line]] while drawing, so all of them update in the same frame, with no notification code. The
class is also [[@MainActor]]: all its state is touched only on the main thread, and background work
has to hop back to it explicitly.

<<LineScope/Model/Session.swift>>=
import AppKit
import ImageIO
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI
import UniformTypeIdentifiers

<<Session: profile and spectrum point types>>

<<Session: the session class>>

<<Session: the focused-value entry>>

<<Session: the active session>>
@

The views plot two kinds of series. A profile keeps the raw channel values (for the spectrum and
the inspector's statistics), the samples they came from, and a list of points for the chart. The
points have integer identifiers because Swift Charts identifies marks, and the sample index is a
natural, stable key.

<<Session: profile and spectrum point types>>=
struct Profile {
    struct Point: Identifiable {
        let id: Int
        let x: Double
        let y: Double
    }

    var points: [Point]
    var values: [Float]
    var samples: LineSamples
}

struct SpectrumPoint: Identifiable {
    let id: Int
    let frequency: Double
    let magnitude: Double
}
