\subsection{State}

<<Session: stored state>>=
<<Session: identity, variants and the line>>

<<Session: analysis settings>>

<<Session: panes, focus and pending work>>
@

The variants can only be changed by the session's own methods, which maintain the invariant that
index 0 is the original and is never removed.

The line is stored in \emph{normalized} coordinates, $(u, v) \in [0,1]^2$ relative to the image
extent. A filtered copy has the same size as its source and a resampled copy is only scaled, so the
same normalized line lands on the same content in every copy, however large it is. This is how a
single line can be ``in the same position of all images, after considering filtering and
resampling'': each pane converts it to its own pixel grid only when it draws or samples.

<<Session: identity, variants and the line>>=
let fileName: String
let sourceInfo: SourceInfo

private(set) var variants: [ImageVariant]
var line: LineSegment = .initial
@

The analysis settings are shared by all panes, deliberately: comparing the red channel of one copy
with the hue of another, or a Hann-windowed spectrum with a rectangular one, would be misleading.
The one subtle setting is the channel index. The color models have different numbers of channels
(RGBA and CMYK have four, HSL three), so switching the model resets an index that would fall
out of range.

<<Session: analysis settings>>=
var colorModel: ColorModel = .rgba {
    didSet { if channelIndex >= colorModel.channels.count { channelIndex = 0 } }
}
var channelIndex = 0
var window: WindowFunction = .hann
var removeMean = true
var logMagnitude = true
var frequencyAxis: FrequencyAxis = .cyclesPerPixel
var useDevicePixels = false
@

The \emph{focused variant} is the one menu operations apply to and the inspector describes: the
last one the user clicked, in any zone. Applying operations to it, rather than always to the original,
is what lets operations chain: blur a copy, then downsample the blurred copy.

[[pendingRequest]] drives the parameter sheet, [[runningOperations]] the progress indicator, and
[[errorMessage]] an alert.

<<Session: panes, focus and pending work>>=
/// Panes per zone, indexed by `Zone.rawValue`.
var panes: [[PaneState]]
/// The variant operations apply to and the inspector describes.
var focusedVariantID: UUID
var pendingRequest: OperationRequest?
private(set) var runningOperations = 0
var errorMessage: String?
@

A new session has just the original, focused, shown in one pane of each zone.

<<Session: initialization>>=
init(document: ImageDocument, fileName: String) {
    let original = ImageVariant(
        buffer: document.buffer, cgImage: document.displayImage,
        parentID: nil, operation: nil, lineage: []
    )
    self.fileName = fileName
    self.sourceInfo = document.info
    self.variants = [original]
    self.focusedVariantID = original.id
    self.panes = Zone.allCases.map { _ in [PaneState(variantID: original.id)] }
}
@

Looking a variant up by identifier is a linear search. A session holds a handful of variants,
and an array keeps the tab order for free. If the focused variant has disappeared, the original
stands in for it, so [[focusedVariant]] never fails.

<<Session: looking up variants>>=
var original: ImageVariant { variants[0] }

var focusedVariant: ImageVariant {
    variant(focusedVariantID) ?? original
}

func variant(_ id: UUID) -> ImageVariant? {
    variants.first { $0.id == id }
}

var channelName: String { colorModel.channels[channelIndex] }
@

\subsection{Panes}

Selecting a tab in a pane both shows that variant there and focuses it. Splitting a zone appends
a pane showing the first variant not yet visible in that zone, which is usually what the user wants
to compare with; if every variant is already shown, the new pane shows the focused one. Unsplitting
removes the last pane, never the only one.

<<Session: selecting and splitting panes>>=
func select(_ variantID: UUID, zone: Zone, pane: Int) {
    panes[zone.rawValue][pane].variantID = variantID
    focusedVariantID = variantID
}

func split(_ zone: Zone) {
    let shown = Set(panes[zone.rawValue].map(\.variantID))
    let next = variants.first { !shown.contains($0.id) } ?? focusedVariant
    panes[zone.rawValue].append(PaneState(variantID: next.id))
}

func unsplit(_ zone: Zone) {
    guard panes[zone.rawValue].count > 1 else { return }
    panes[zone.rawValue].removeLast()
}
@

\subsection{Variants and operations}

Closing a variant removes its tab. The original can't be closed. Panes that showed the closed
variant fall back to the original, and so does the focus. Variants derived from the closed one keep
working, since each carries its own lineage.

<<Session: closing a variant>>=
func closeVariant(_ id: UUID) {
    guard let index = variants.firstIndex(where: { $0.id == id }), index > 0 else { return }
    variants.remove(at: index)
    for z in panes.indices {
        for p in panes[z].indices where panes[z][p].variantID == id {
            panes[z][p].variantID = original.id
        }
    }
    if focusedVariantID == id { focusedVariantID = original.id }
}
@

Performing an operation must not freeze the window: resampling a large photograph with a wide
kernel takes a noticeable fraction of a second. The session captures the source variant and its
buffer on the main actor, then starts a task. The task itself inherits the main actor, but the
heavy call runs in a \emph{detached} task at user-initiated priority, which runs on a background
thread. This is safe because [[apply]] is a pure function of a [[Sendable]] buffer
(Section~\ref{sec:operations}). When the result comes back, the rest of the task runs on the main
actor again: it renders the image, builds the variant with its extended lineage, appends it and
shows it.

The source is captured \emph{before} the task starts, so the operation applies to the variant that
was focused when the user chose the command, even if the focus moves while it is running.

<<Session: performing an operation in the background>>=
/// Applies an operation to the focused variant in the background and adds the result as a new tab.
func perform(_ operation: ImageOperation) {
    let source = focusedVariant
    let input = source.buffer
    runningOperations += 1
    Task {
        let output = await Task.detached(priority: .userInitiated) {
            operation.apply(to: input)
        }.value
        runningOperations -= 1
        guard let cg = output.makeCGImage() else {
            errorMessage = "Could not render the result of \(operation.displayName)."
            return
        }
        let variant = ImageVariant(
            buffer: output, cgImage: cg, parentID: source.id,
            operation: operation, lineage: source.lineage + [operation]
        )
        variants.append(variant)
        show(variant.id)
    }
}
@

Where should a new variant appear? When a zone is split, the user is usually comparing: the left
pane holds the baseline and the right pane the candidate. So the new variant goes into the
\emph{last} pane of each zone, which is also the only pane when the zone is not split. It becomes
the focus, so the next operation chains from it.

<<Session: showing a new variant>>=
/// Shows a new variant in every zone: in the last pane when split (so the left pane keeps the
/// comparison baseline), otherwise in the only pane.
private func show(_ id: UUID) {
    for z in panes.indices {
        panes[z][panes[z].count - 1].variantID = id
    }
    focusedVariantID = id
}
@

\subsection{Analysis}

A profile samples the line in the variant's own pixel grid (Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis}) and extracts
the chosen channel of the chosen color model from every sample. The chart's $x$ coordinate is
the normalized position along the line, not the sample index. A half-size copy has half as many
samples, and with positions in $[0,1]$ its profile still lines up with the original's.

These functions are called from view bodies, so they run again on every change of the line or the
settings. That is cheap: a few thousand bilinear samples and one FFT.

<<Session: computing a profile>>=
func profile(for variant: ImageVariant) -> Profile {
    let samples = LineSampler.sample(variant.buffer, along: line)
    let values = samples.colors.map {
        ColorConversion.value(of: $0, model: colorModel, channel: channelIndex)
    }
    let points = values.indices.map { Profile.Point(id: $0, x: samples.positions[$0], y: Double(values[$0])) }
    return Profile(points: points, values: values, samples: samples)
}
@

The spectrum of a profile is computed with the current window and mean setting. The sample spacing
is guarded away from zero, which it would be for a line of zero length. For plotting, each bin gets
its frequency on the chosen axis, and its amplitude, optionally in decibels,
\[
  A_{\mathrm{dB}} = \max\bigl(20\log_{10} A,\ -100\bigr).
\]
The floor keeps empty bins, whose logarithm would be $-\infty$, on the chart.

<<Session: computing a spectrum>>=
func spectrum(for profile: Profile) -> SpectrumResult {
    SpectrumAnalyzer.compute(
        profile.values, spacing: max(profile.samples.spacing, 1e-9),
        window: window, removeMean: removeMean
    )
}

func spectrumPoints(_ s: SpectrumResult) -> [SpectrumPoint] {
    let freqs = frequencyAxis == .cyclesPerPixel ? s.cyclesPerPixel : s.cyclesPerLine
    return s.amplitudes.indices.map { i in
        let a = s.amplitudes[i]
        return SpectrumPoint(
            id: i, frequency: freqs[i],
            magnitude: logMagnitude ? max(20 * log10(max(a, 1e-12)), Self.dBFloor) : a
        )
    }
}

static let dBFloor = -100.0
@

\subsection{Export}\label{sec:export}

Exporting writes the focused variant as a PNG. The proposed name combines the file's base name with
the variant's lineage, with the arrows and slashes that can't appear in a file name replaced. The
image written is the variant's display image, which is exactly what is on screen: 8-bit, sRGB.

<<Session: exporting the focused variant>>=
func exportFocused() {
    let variant = focusedVariant
    let panel = NSSavePanel()
    panel.allowedContentTypes = [.png]
    let base = (fileName as NSString).deletingPathExtension
    let suffix = variant.isOriginal ? "original" : variant.name
        .replacingOccurrences(of: " → ", with: "_")
        .replacingOccurrences(of: "/", with: "-")
    panel.nameFieldStringValue = "\(base)-\(suffix).png"
    guard panel.runModal() == .OK, let url = panel.url else { return }
    guard
        let dest = CGImageDestinationCreateWithURL(url as CFURL, UTType.png.identifier as CFString, 1, nil)
    else {
        errorMessage = "Could not create \(url.lastPathComponent)."
        return
    }
    CGImageDestinationAddImage(dest, variant.cgImage, nil)
    if !CGImageDestinationFinalize(dest) {
        errorMessage = "Could not write \(url.lastPathComponent)."
    }
}
@

\subsection{Finding the session from outside the window}

Menus must act on the session of the window the user is working in. SwiftUI's \emph{focused
values} provide exactly this: a window publishes its session, and the menu commands read the
value of the key window. The entry is declared with the [[@Entry]] macro.

<<Session: the focused-value entry>>=
extension FocusedValues {
    @Entry var session: Session?
}
@

Focused values fail in one case, and it matters here: the inspector. While the user interacts with
the inspector window, \emph{it} is the key window, and it has no session, so a focused value would
be [[nil]] just when the inspector needs it. The app therefore also keeps track of the session of
the most recently key \emph{document} window, in a shared observable object. Document windows
update it as they become key (Chapter~\ref{ch:views}), the inspector reads it, and the menus use it
as a fallback.

<<Session: the active session>>=
/// The session of the most recently key document window, for the inspector window (which is itself
/// key while being used, so focused values would not reach it).
@MainActor @Observable
final class ActiveSession {
    static let shared = ActiveSession()
    var session: Session?
}
@
